# Rivet's kernel requirements — the fragment `ADR-024` §5 rules for.
#
# This file is a *fragment*, merged over the Raspberry Pi `bcm2711_defconfig`,
# and that is the whole design: a full `.config` is several thousand lines in
# which a meaningful change and noise are indistinguishable in a diff, which is
# exactly how `ADR-002` §5.3's recorded risk materialises — a Stage 1 dependency
# dropped by someone tidying. A fragment is short enough that a reviewer reads
# all of it.
#
# **`ADR-024` §3 is the single authority for what belongs here.** Every line
# below traces to a row of that table, and the comment names the row. Do not add
# a flag here because something needed it; add the requirement to §3 first, then
# reflect it here. Seven partially-maintained flag lists is how `BCMGENET` gets
# dropped (`ADR-024` §2).
#
# `ADR-024` §4 rules `=y` throughout and never `=m`. Every line here obeys that.
# The *base* defconfig still carries many `=m` symbols that Rivet does not
# depend on; stripping those is §8's "strip when it works" pass and is not this
# fragment's job.

# --- ADR-024 §3: DEVTMPFS, DEVTMPFS_MOUNT — ADR-005 §2.1 -------------------
# There is no udev in this system. The kernel populates /dev itself and mounts
# it, or PID 1 comes up with no /dev/console and the boot is indistinguishable
# from a hang (the failure ADR-026 §3 cost an iteration to find).
CONFIG_DEVTMPFS=y
CONFIG_DEVTMPFS_MOUNT=y

# --- ADR-024 §3: BLK_DEV_INITRD -------------------------------------------
# The development loop boots PID 1 out of an initramfs with no disk, no
# partition table and no bootloader (`ADR-026` §3). Without RD_GZIP the kernel
# accepts the cpio and silently ignores it, then panics for want of an init.
CONFIG_BLK_DEV_INITRD=y
CONFIG_RD_GZIP=y

# --- ADR-024 §3: BCMGENET — ADR-004 §2.3 ----------------------------------
# eth0, the Stage 1 bring-up scaffold. `ADR-002` §5.2 recorded the specific risk
# that this gets dropped when the config is tidied; it is named here so the
# tidying has to argue with a comment.
CONFIG_NET_VENDOR_BROADCOM=y
CONFIG_BCMGENET=y

# --- ADR-024 §3: BRCMFMAC + SDIO — ADR-004 §8 ------------------------------
# Wireless. The CYW43455 is on SDIO, not USB or PCIe, on this board.
#
# RFKILL is not in §3, and it is here because leaving it out silently broke
# four §3 requirements. Observed 2026-08-23: with the base defconfig's
# `CONFIG_RFKILL=m`, kconfig demoted CFG80211 back to `=m` after the merge —
# a tristate cannot be `y` while something it needs is `m` — and MAC80211,
# BRCMUTIL and BRCMFMAC followed it down. `merge_config.sh` reported no error,
# because from its point of view the lines were applied; the demotion happened
# afterwards, in `olddefconfig`.
#
# That is precisely the failure `build.sh --show-config` exists to catch, and
# it is why reading the produced `.config` back is not optional: the fragment
# said `=y` four times and the kernel disagreed four times, in silence.
CONFIG_RFKILL=y
CONFIG_CFG80211=y
CONFIG_MAC80211=y
CONFIG_BRCMUTIL=y
CONFIG_BRCMFMAC=y
CONFIG_BRCMFMAC_SDIO=y

# --- The firmware goes IN the kernel, and this is not an optimisation -------
#
# **Measured on the board, 2026-08-30** (`PLAN.md` §102.25.5). brcmfmac probes
# when the SDIO card enumerates, and that is BEFORE the root filesystem is
# mounted:
#
#   2.7515  brcmfmac: Direct firmware load ... failed with error -2
#   2.8703  VFS: Mounted root (ext4 filesystem) readonly on device 179:4
#
# 119 milliseconds. The file was on the card, under exactly the name the driver
# asked for, and did not exist yet. Every other distribution survives this by
# making brcmfmac a MODULE that udev loads long after root is up; `ADR-008` has
# no initramfs, no udev and no modules, so the only place the firmware can be is
# the kernel image itself.
#
# The names are `ADR-004` §8.1's, measured off a real board: the driver appends
# the first device-tree compatible string to the base name. `regulatory.db` is
# here for the same reason and failed 100 ms earlier.
#
# **The directory is RELATIVE to the kernel source tree**, which is
# `$(BUILD_DIR)/linux-custom`, so `../../target` is Buildroot's target
# directory. An absolute path would encode where the output tree is mounted,
# and it is `/br` only inside the container.
#
# `tools/buildroot/external.mk` makes the kernel wait for the firmware package;
# Buildroot's own list does not include the Raspberry Pi one, and a kernel built
# before those files exist carries no firmware and says nothing.
CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE_DIR="../../target/lib/firmware"
CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE="brcm/brcmfmac43455-sdio.raspberrypi,4-model-b.bin brcm/brcmfmac43455-sdio.raspberrypi,4-model-b.txt brcm/brcmfmac43455-sdio.raspberrypi,4-model-b.clm_blob regulatory.db regulatory.db.p7s"

# --- ADR-024 §3: FW_LOADER — ADR-004 §8 ------------------------------------
# The kernel reads /lib/firmware directly. `ADR-005` §2.1: it has never needed a
# userspace helper, and Rivet is not going to give it one.
CONFIG_FW_LOADER=y
# CONFIG_FW_LOADER_USER_HELPER is not set

# --- ADR-024 §3: MMC / SDHCI ----------------------------------------------
# Reaching the root filesystem at all.
CONFIG_MMC=y
CONFIG_MMC_BLOCK=y
CONFIG_MMC_SDHCI=y
CONFIG_MMC_SDHCI_PLTFM=y
CONFIG_MMC_SDHCI_IPROC=y

# --- ADR-024 §3: filesystems ----------------------------------------------
# EXT4_FS   — /state (ADR-018 §7)
# VFAT_FS   — the boot partition is FAT32 (ADR-018 §4) and PID 1 mounts it to
#             read the provisioning file. Added to §3 on 2026-08-22 by
#             implementing ADR-017 §3.1.5, not by review.
# EROFS_FS  — /system, read-only *by format* (ADR-018 §3)
# TMPFS     — /run and the /system/ext mount point (ADR-016 §3, ADR-018 §6)
CONFIG_EXT4_FS=y
CONFIG_VFAT_FS=y
CONFIG_EROFS_FS=y
CONFIG_TMPFS=y
# vfat refuses to mount without a codepage and an IO charset compiled in. This
# is not in §3 because §3 names capabilities; it is the dependency that makes
# VFAT_FS actually usable, and leaving it out produces a mount that fails at
# boot with an error about the NLS rather than about the filesystem.
CONFIG_NLS=y
CONFIG_NLS_CODEPAGE_437=y
CONFIG_NLS_ISO8859_1=y
CONFIG_FAT_DEFAULT_CODEPAGE=437
CONFIG_FAT_DEFAULT_IOCHARSET="iso8859-1"

# --- ADR-024 §3: BLK_DEV_LOOP — ADR-018 §6 --------------------------------
# Extensions are loop-mounted image files.
CONFIG_BLK_DEV_LOOP=y

# --- ADR-024 §3: CGROUPS, cgroup v2 — ADR-015 §5.1, ADR-017 §3.1.3 --------
# Resource limits, and the authority for what is running.
#
# **The memory controller is the point of this block.** `ADR-024` §8.1 item 4
# was filed as a command-line accident, then CORRECTED on 2026-08-23 when
# Rivet booted its own image with its own cmdline.txt — which carries no
# cgroup_disable=memory — and PID 1 still read `cpuset cpu io pids` out of
# /sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.controllers. Memory limits accepted and silently
# ignored is the failure this line prevents.
#
# **The kernel-config hypothesis does not survive contact with the tree, 2026-08-23.**
# §8.1's correction named the memory-controller symbol as the leading candidate.
# Building this fragment showed `merge_config.sh` reporting its *previous* value
# — the one the Raspberry Pi `bcm2711_defconfig` already carries — as `=y`. It
# is therefore already enabled in the defconfig the stock kernel is built from,
# and this line changes nothing about it. See `ADR-024` §8.2 for where that
# leaves the question.
#
# Kept anyway, and deliberately: §3 is the authority for what Rivet *depends*
# on, and depending on a symbol the base happens to set is exactly the case §2
# exists for — the base can change, and this file is what would notice.
CONFIG_CGROUPS=y
CONFIG_MEMCG=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_PIDS=y
CONFIG_CPUSETS=y
CONFIG_BLK_CGROUP=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_SCHED=y
CONFIG_FAIR_GROUP_SCHED=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_FREEZER=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_DEVICE=y

# --- ADR-024 §3: SECURITY_LANDLOCK — ADR-015 §5.1 -------------------------
# The flag that decided the schedule. `ADR-024` §8.1 item 1: the stock kernel
# does not have it, /sys/kernel/security/lsm reads `capability` and nothing
# else, and ADR-015's sandbox therefore has no kernel to run on.
#
# The LSM list below is the second half and is easy to miss. §8.1's own method
# note says it: a compiled-in LSM still has to appear in the built-in list (or a
# boot-time `lsm=`) to do anything at all. Landlock compiled but unlisted would
# leave /sys/kernel/security/lsm without it and every landlock call would fail —
# a sandbox that looks configured and confines nothing.
#
# The Pi defconfig's value is the empty string, observed 2026-08-23 in
# merge_config.sh's report of the previous value. That is consistent with §8.1's
# runtime reading of `capability` and nothing else.
CONFIG_SECURITY=y
CONFIG_SECURITY_LANDLOCK=y
CONFIG_SECURITYFS=y
CONFIG_LSM="landlock,lockdown,yama,loadpin,safesetid,bpf"

# --- ADR-024 §3: SECCOMP, SECCOMP_FILTER — ADR-015 §6 ---------------------
# A later enforcement stage, declared now.
CONFIG_SECCOMP=y

# --- ADR-024 §3: namespaces — ADR-015 §5.1, §5.3 --------------------------
# NET_NS, PID_NS and mount namespaces are the sandbox. Mount namespaces are
# unconditional in the kernel and so have no symbol of their own.
CONFIG_NAMESPACES=y
CONFIG_NET_NS=y
CONFIG_PID_NS=y
CONFIG_UTS_NS=y
CONFIG_IPC_NS=y

# --- ADR-024 §3.1: user namespaces are deliberately OFF -------------------
# Not an omission — a decision, recorded so nobody enables it later to make
# something work without understanding what it costs. Unprivileged user
# namespaces are a well-known privilege-escalation surface, and Rivet does not
# need them: rivet-sysd runs as root and creates namespaces on
# applications' behalf (`ADR-017` §3.1). The hardening win is free *because* of
# how supervision was structured, and the base defconfig sets it to `=y`
# (observed 2026-08-23), which is `ADR-024` §8.1 item 2 confirmed from the
# source side rather than from a running board.
#
# The line below is kconfig's "not set" form and is load bearing, not a comment.
# CONFIG_USER_NS is not set

# --- ADR-024 §3: NF_TABLES, NF_TABLES_INET, NF_CONNTRACK, VETH ------------
# ADR-023 §2/§7 want the generated ruleset in both address families; §3.1 says
# without conntrack `egress` never receives replies; §8 wants VETH only if group
# namespaces ever need egress.
CONFIG_NET=y

# --- AF_PACKET, for DHCP (ADR-004 §8) -------------------------------------
# Moved here from linux/qemu-virt.config on 2026-08-30. A DHCP client cannot
# use a UDP socket to ask for its first address: it has no address yet, and
# the server answers before one is assigned. DISCOVER and REQUEST therefore go
# out over a raw AF_PACKET socket, which is why this is a product requirement
# and not a convenience of the QEMU loop.
#
# It was in the dev-loop fragment because that is where reaching a guest over
# `-netdev user` first needed it, and both fragments merge into one kernel --
# so nothing was broken and nothing would have been, until ADR-024 §8's
# stripping pass removed the file the product depended on.
CONFIG_PACKET=y

CONFIG_NETFILTER=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_ADVANCED=y
CONFIG_NF_CONNTRACK=y
CONFIG_NF_TABLES=y
CONFIG_NF_TABLES_INET=y
CONFIG_VETH=y
# NOT in §3, and flagged rather than smuggled: NF_CONNTRACK is the subsystem,
# but `nft` cannot match on connection state without the `ct` expression. A
# ruleset written to ADR-023 §3.1 would load and then never match a reply.
# Recommended as a §3 addition; recorded here in the meantime.
CONFIG_NFT_CT=y

# --- ADR-024 §3: HW_RANDOM_BCM2835 — ADR-005 §6, ADR-024 §6 ---------------
# Entropy for host keys at first boot. §6 names the trap precisely: an
# *uncredited* source fills the pool without raising the entropy estimate, so
# generation still blocks or proceeds weakly. Enabled is only half of it.
CONFIG_HW_RANDOM=y
CONFIG_HW_RANDOM_BCM2835=y
#
# --- CORRECTED 2026-08-23: BCM2835 is the wrong driver for this board -------
#
# The line above was right for a Pi 3 and wrong for the target hardware, and it
# was wrong in the direction that fails silently. `ADR-024` §3's requirements
# table names `HW_RANDOM_BCM2835`; the Raspberry Pi 4 is a **BCM2711**, whose
# RNG is an RNG200 block, and the preprocessed `bcm2711-rpi-4-b.dts` in the
# kernel tree this file configures says so:
#
#   rng@7e104000 {
#           compatible = "brcm,bcm2711-rng200";
#           reg = <0x7e104000 0x28>;
#   };
#
# `drivers/char/hw_random/iproc-rng200.c`'s `of_match` table carries exactly
# that string. So the driver is IPROC_RNG200, and with BCM2835 alone this board
# would have **no hwrng at all** — which is not an error at boot, just an
# entropy source that never appears.
#
# It happens to be `=y` in the built `.config` already, because Buildroot's Pi 4
# defconfig selects it. That is the defconfig being right, not this fragment:
# `ADR-024` §5 makes this file Rivet's *statement of requirement*, and §7 has
# the running system check §3's list at boot. A requirement that names the wrong
# symbol checks the wrong symbol.
CONFIG_HW_RANDOM_IPROC_RNG200=y
#
# --- And the crediting half, answered 2026-08-23 rather than assumed --------
#
# `ADR-024` §6 warns that an enabled-but-uncredited source leaves generation
# blocking or weak. On **Linux 6.12.61** — the tree `tools/buildroot/build.sh`
# builds — that is no longer the default behaviour, and both crediting paths
# were read out of the source rather than remembered:
#
#   drivers/char/hw_random/core.c:46   default_quality = 1024  /* maximum */
#   drivers/char/hw_random/core.c:538  rng->quality = min(min(default_quality,
#                                        1024), rng->quality ?: 1024)
#   drivers/char/hw_random/core.c:503  entropy = rc * quality * 8 + credit
#
# Neither bcm2835-rng.c nor iproc-rng200.c sets a `.quality` of its own, so both
# take 1024 and every byte read is credited in full.
#
#   drivers/of/fdt.c:1038              add_bootloader_randomness(rng_seed, l)
#   drivers/char/random.c:822          static bool trust_bootloader = true
#   drivers/char/random.c:987          if (trust_bootloader) credit_init_bits(len*8)
#
# `CONFIG_RANDOM_TRUST_BOOTLOADER` **does not exist in this tree** — checked
# two ways, a grep over every `Kconfig*` and a grep over the whole tree, both
# empty — because upstream removed the option and made trust the default. So the
# `/chosen/rng-seed` the Raspberry Pi firmware inserts is credited too, and
# there is no symbol to add here for either path.
#
# What there IS to do is a negative, and it belongs to `cmd/rivet-image`'s
# `boot` module rather than to this file: **never put
# `random.trust_bootloader=0` or `rng_core.default_quality=0` in cmdline.txt.**
# Both would reinstate exactly the stall §6 describes, from a file that looks
# like tuning.

# --- ADR-024 §3: IKCONFIG, IKCONFIG_PROC — ADR-024 §7 ---------------------
# So the running kernel can report its own configuration and Rivet can check
# §3's requirements at boot and name the ones that are absent.
#
# §8.1 item 3 is the reason both are here as `=y`: on the stock kernel
# IKCONFIG=m with the module unloaded, so /proc/config.gz did not exist despite
# IKCONFIG_PROC=y, and §7's self-report would have had nothing to read.
CONFIG_IKCONFIG=y
CONFIG_IKCONFIG_PROC=y


# --- Graphics and input, for kiosk mode: ADR-015 §6.2, added 2026-08-31 -----
#
# **This is §102.25's finding in a second subsystem.** That one was: Pi OS's
# kernel had a MODULAR wireless stack, a Rivet image has no /lib/modules, and
# /sys/bus/sdio/drivers answered `entry_count: 0` -- no driver on the bus at
# all. Read out of /br/build/linux-custom/.config on 2026-08-31, which is the
# configuration the running kernel was actually built from:
#
#     CONFIG_DRM=m   CONFIG_DRM_V3D=m   CONFIG_DRM_VC4=m
#     CONFIG_INPUT_JOYDEV=m   CONFIG_JOYSTICK_XPAD=m
#
# All modules, from the Pi kernel's `bcm2711` defconfig -- and **this file had
# no display or joystick option at all**, so nothing here ever asked either
# way. On a board that means /sys/class/drm absent, /dev/dri absent, and no GPU
# as far as userland is concerned. What makes the splash work is
# CONFIG_FB_SIMPLE, which the base defconfig builds IN.
#
# Rivet loads no modules and ships no /lib/modules, so `=m` is `absent` here.
#
# **What this does NOT do.** DRM in the kernel is the first of several things a
# GPU application needs, and Mesa for aarch64-musl is the expensive one and is
# not built (`PLAN.md` §104.6). This makes /dev/dri EXIST. `ADR-003` §6.2's
# distinction governs: it answers *can the device be opened*, never *will
# anything draw*.
CONFIG_DRM=y
CONFIG_DRM_V3D=y

# **VC4 needs sound, and asking for it without this SILENTLY gave `=m`.**
#
# Found by verifying rather than assuming, which is what
# `tools/buildroot/build.sh --show-config` exists for. The first attempt set
# `CONFIG_DRM_VC4=y` alone; the merged configuration came back
#
#     CONFIG_DRM=y   CONFIG_DRM_V3D=y   CONFIG_DRM_VC4=m
#
# because `drivers/gpu/drm/vc4/Kconfig` has `depends on SND && SND_SOC`, both
# of which are `=m` in the `bcm2711` defconfig, and **a tristate cannot be `y`
# when a dependency is `m`.** Kconfig does not refuse that; it downgrades, and
# a downgraded symbol on a machine with no /lib/modules is an absent driver.
#
# The dependency is real rather than incidental: VC4 is the display controller
# and it carries HDMI **audio**, so ASoC is part of the device it drives.
# Sound built in is therefore not scope creep here — it is what asking for HDMI
# on this SoC means, and an infotainment display is one of the things kiosk
# mode is for.
#
# **VC4 is the half that matters for a screen.** V3D is the GPU and gives a
# render node; VC4 is KMS and scanout. Without it there is no /dev/dri/card0,
# which is the node a compositor or a DRM-direct renderer opens.
CONFIG_SND=y
CONFIG_SND_SOC=y
CONFIG_DRM_VC4=y

# **Gamepads, because kiosk mode is for games as well as displays.** Aura's
# renderer takes no input at all, and optimising this list for it would be
# optimising for the sample of one -- the maintainer named games explicitly
# when the shape of kiosk mode was ruled.
#
# Everything else input-shaped is ALREADY built in, and that was measured
# rather than assumed: INPUT_EVDEV, INPUT_TOUCHSCREEN, INPUT_MOUSEDEV, HID,
# HID_GENERIC, USB_HID and HIDRAW are all `=y` in the base defconfig, and a
# board answered with /dev/input/event0..3 before any of this was written.
# These two are the only input options that were modules.
#
# JOYDEV is the legacy /dev/js* interface. Modern consumers use evdev --
# Raylib's DRM backend among them -- so this is for software that has not moved.
CONFIG_INPUT_JOYDEV=y
CONFIG_JOYSTICK_XPAD=y

# **The HDMI DDC bus, and the reason VC4 bound nothing for a whole day.**
#
# `CONFIG_I2C_BRCMSTB` was `=m` in the base defconfig. `PLAN.md` §104.11 is
# what that cost, and it is `§102.25`'s finding for the THIRD time in this
# file: the Pi defconfig builds it as a module, Rivet ships no /lib/modules and
# loads none, so the capability is silently absent on a board.
#
# **Silently is the word.** `vc4_hdmi_bind` resolves its `ddc` phandle to an
# I2C adapter and, when there is none, does this:
#
#     vc4_hdmi->ddc = of_find_i2c_adapter_by_node(ddc_node);
#     if (!vc4_hdmi->ddc)
#             return -EPROBE_DEFER;
#
# **No message.** Not `dev_err_probe`, not a `drm_err` — the line above it
# logs when the *node* is missing, and this one, when the *driver* is, says
# nothing at all. So the component master retried sixteen times over eleven
# seconds, bound `fe400000.hvs` and stopped, and wrote not one word at `err` or
# `warn` in the entire boot. Three hypotheses were formed and discarded against
# that silence before the answer was read out of the source and this file.
#
# `ddc0` is `/soc/i2c@7ef04500` and `ddc1` is `/soc/i2c@7ef09500` in
# `bcm2711-rpi-4-b.dtb` — both `brcm,bcm2711-hdmi-i2c`, both this driver's.
#
# **The other I2C controllers are deliberately left as modules.**
# `I2C_BCM2835` drives the GPIO header's buses, which nothing on a Rivet uses
# yet; this is the one the display needs. Adding only what is needed is the
# same discipline the DRM block above follows.
CONFIG_I2C_BRCMSTB=y


# --- Bluetooth, TIER 1 ONLY: a BLE observer. ADR-041, ADR-024 §3 -----------
#
# **`§102.25`'s finding for the FOURTH time in this file, and this one shipped
# on every card this project has ever written.** Read out of
# `/br/build/linux-custom/.config` on 2026-09-05, which is the configuration
# the running kernel was built from:
#
#     CONFIG_BT=m  CONFIG_BT_HCIUART=m  CONFIG_BT_HCIVHCI=m  CONFIG_BT_HIDP=m
#     CONFIG_BT_RFCOMM=m  CONFIG_BT_BNEP=m  CONFIG_BT_HCIBTUSB=m
#
# All of it inherited from the Broadcom `bcm2711` defconfig, all of it `=m`, and
# `ADR-024` §4 rules everything built in — a Rivet has no `/lib/modules` and no
# module loader. Read off the test board the same day, `/proc/modules` is EMPTY
# and `/sys/class/bluetooth` is ABSENT. So the whole stack has been compiled
# into every image, installed nowhere, and been unreachable.
#
# **What this block asks for is the observer and nothing else** — `.rulings/`
# R-144: *reading advertisements* is the whole ruled capability. GATT central
# and Classic HID are DEFERRED, and R-144.1 item 4 forbids building for them,
# so every symbol the 2026-09-05 study enabled for those two tiers is left out
# here. `docs/ADR-024` §3's table carries the row-by-row justification and
# `ADR-041` §5 carries what was omitted and what omitting it costs.

# The subsystem. `net/bluetooth`'s `select`s pull CRC16, CRYPTO_CMAC,
# CRYPTO_ECDH and the rest up with it; they are not listed because listing a
# symbol kconfig guarantees would be a second statement of one fact
# (`ADR-011`), and `--show-config` reads the result back either way.
CONFIG_BT=y

# LE, which IS tier 1. An advertisement is an LE PDU and nothing else here
# reads one.
CONFIG_BT_LE=y

# **BR/EDR is deliberately OFF, and this line is load bearing.**
#
# The base defconfig sets `CONFIG_BT_BREDR=y`, so leaving this out would ship
# Bluetooth Classic — SDP, BR/EDR L2CAP, and with them the `=m` RFCOMM, BNEP
# and HIDP symbols above, still unreachable and now attached to a subsystem
# that IS reachable. That is the deferred tier 3 arriving as untested surface
# for a capability nobody ruled (R-144.1 item 4), which is the one thing the
# ruling asks this work not to do.
#
# Turning it off is not a rejection of tier 3: `docs/design-candidates/
# Bluetooth.md` §7.3 is the roadmap for it and this is the line it changes back.
# CONFIG_BT_BREDR is not set

# --- the transport the CYW43455 is actually on ------------------------------
#
# The Pi 4's Bluetooth half is on a UART behind serdev, not USB and not SDIO —
# which is why `BT_HCIUART_SERDEV` and the serial device bus are here and
# `BT_HCIBTUSB` is not (`ADR-041` §5.2). `BT_HCIUART_BCM` selects
# `BT_HCIUART_H4` and `FW_LOADER`; H4 is named anyway because it is the frame
# format on the wire and a reader should not have to know that one option
# quietly implies another.
#
# **The firmware this driver asks for is NOT compiled into the kernel**, unlike
# the wireless half above, and the difference is a measurement rather than a
# preference. `brcmfmac` probes when the SDIO card enumerates, 119 ms before
# the root filesystem is mounted, so its firmware has nowhere to be but the
# image. `hci_bcm`'s patchram is requested from `hdev->setup`, which runs at
# `hci_dev_open` — when `rivet-bluetoothd` opens the controller, long after
# `/system` is mounted. So it can be an ordinary file, and it is one:
# `BR2_PACKAGE_BRCMFMAC_SDIO_FIRMWARE_RPI_BT` in the defconfig.
#
# **And the name it asks for has not been read off a board.** `AGENTS.md` §5:
# firmware filenames are copied from a running system, never from
# documentation. `docs/design-candidates/Bluetooth.md` §3.4 is where the
# documentation-derived name is recorded as such, and one boot closes it.
CONFIG_SERIAL_DEV_BUS=y
CONFIG_SERIAL_DEV_CTRL_TTYPORT=y
CONFIG_BT_HCIUART=y
CONFIG_BT_HCIUART_SERDEV=y
CONFIG_BT_HCIUART_H4=y
CONFIG_BT_HCIUART_BCM=y
